\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Wage Coordination and Export Performance\\ in Small Open Economies}
\author{M.~Duarte}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
The literature on small open economies has long associated
coordinated wage bargaining with disciplined cost growth
and, through it, with export competitiveness.
\cite{hale2019} provides the sharpest statement of the
sectoral mechanism: where export-sector unions set the pace,
wage moderation follows the exposed sector's productivity
rather than the sheltered sector's aspirations.

\section{Institutional Background}
The classic accounts of postwar wage moderation emphasise
the centralisation of bargaining \cite{oconnor2015},
but centralisation alone does not explain why some
decentralised systems sustained coordination.
Recent work stresses the informational role of pattern
bargaining, in which a leading sector's settlement anchors
expectations across the economy \cite{delacroix2021}.

\section{Discussion}
Taken together, the sectoral and the informational accounts
suggest that coordination is a property of bargaining
networks rather than of formal centralisation alone.
The export-performance consequences then depend on which
sector occupies the pivotal position in the network.

\section{Conclusion}
Wage coordination in small open economies is best understood
as an emergent property of intersectoral bargaining,
and its export effects follow from the structure of the
network rather than from any single institutional trait.

